{"id":"mirror-a-tree","lc":"invert-binary-tree","title":"Mirror a Tree","diff":"easy","pattern":"trees","noted":true,"tags":["binary-tree","dfs","bfs"],"statement":"<p>You get the <code>root</code> of a binary tree. Turn it into its mirror image: at <b>every</b> node, the left child and the right child swap places. Return the root.</p>\n<p>Trees in the tests are written in level order, with <code>null</code> for a missing child: <code>[8, 3, 10, null, 6]</code> is 8 with children 3 and 10, where 3 has only a right child 6. Trailing <code>null</code>s are left out. An empty tree is <code>null</code>, shown as <code>[]</code>.</p>","examples":[{"in":"root = [8, 3, 10, null, 6, 9]","out":"[8, 10, 3, null, 9, 6]","why":"Not only the root's children swap. 3 had its child on the right and now has it on the left; 10 had 9 on the left and now has it on the right."},{"in":"root = [1, 2]","out":"[1, null, 2]","why":"A lone left child becomes a lone right child. The null slot moves too."},{"in":"root = []","out":"[]","why":"The mirror of an empty tree is empty. Return null."}],"constraints":["0 ≤ number of nodes ≤ 20 000","-1000 ≤ Node.val ≤ 1000","The tree is at most 500 levels deep"],"fn":{"name":"mirrorTree","params":[["root","TreeNode"]],"returns":"TreeNode"},"tests":[{"args":[[8,3,10,null,6,9]],"expected":[8,10,3,null,9,6]},{"args":[[1,2]],"expected":[1,null,2]},{"args":[[]],"expected":[]},{"args":[[7]],"expected":[7]},{"args":[[4,null,5]],"expected":[4,5]},{"args":[[2,1,3]],"expected":[2,3,1]},{"args":[[1,2,3,4,5,6,7]],"expected":[1,3,2,7,6,5,4]},{"args":[[0,0,null,0]],"expected":[0,null,0,null,0]},{"args":[[5,5,5,5,null,null,5]],"expected":[5,5,5,5,null,null,5]},{"args":[[-1000,1000,-3]],"expected":[-1000,-3,1000]},{"args":[[6,2,9,null,null,7,11,null,8]],"expected":[6,9,2,11,7,null,null,null,null,8]},{"args":[[1,2,null,3,null,4,null,5,null,6,null,7,null,8,null,9,null,10,null,11,null,12,null,13,null,14,null,15,null,16,null,17,null,18,null,19,null,20,null,21,null,22,null,